# 21. Definition of a High-Quality Execution Contract

A high-quality contract does not attempt to predict every action a builder will take.

It creates enough structure that consequential decisions are not left implicit.

A fresh builder should be able to:

- understand the objective;
- select the correct context;
- trust the correct sources;
- determine whether execution may begin;
- activate one bounded task;
- stay inside its permitted scope;
- distinguish implementation from verification;
- classify and repair failures within a budget;
- stop rather than loop forever;
- escalate decisions it does not own;
- recover without destroying unrelated valid work;
- prove success with current evidence;
- persist enough state for another session to continue.

The best contract is therefore not the longest contract.

It is the **smallest contract that makes the important control flow explicit, verifiable, and recoverable without inventing nonexistent mechanisms.**
